      "question": {
        ":question/id": "eb68afad-979d-5067-bbdf-a0270d1ebbf5",
        ":question/problem": "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ x & =2\\cdot 0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(0,2).$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
